00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:02.838
&gt;&gt; So, so can you tell us a little bit
about what you did differently

00:06:02.838 --> 00:06:04.208
or how you, how you, how you helped

00:06:04.208 --> 00:06:06.430
Renault help itself, or help Nissan help
itself?

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:07.330
&gt;&gt; Well let me tell you.

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:11.560
Let me the, the, no matter what is company
which is in trouble my experience shows me

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:14.520
that you don't need to look for the
solution

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:16.690
outside it's inside, it is, this is always
inside.

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:19.850
You just need to look for it, obviously
solution is not unique.

00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:25.910
It's a bit of specific opportunities that
you're going to transform into a plan

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:31.410
with priorities, with a timeframe, with
specific goals and with achievements.

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:32.690
So I was just looking for it.

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:35.114
I knew it was inside, I was looking for
it.

00:06:35.114 --> 00:06:38.221
And obviously the best way to look for it
is interview

00:06:38.221 --> 00:06:42.819
as many people as possible, particularly
people in critical process area.

00:06:42.819 --> 00:06:44.674
That's why you need to know a little bit
of

00:06:44.674 --> 00:06:47.696
industry, to know where you looking, where
you're looking for.

00:06:47.696 --> 00:06:53.252
Let me give you an example, 50% of the PNL
of the car company is purchasing.

00:06:53.252 --> 00:07:00.211
You know, I mean a Nissan has in 100 and
10 billion dollars sales a year.

00:07:00.211 --> 00:07:04.280
From the 110 billion dollars sales, you
have about 55 billion dollars of buying.

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:07.450
We buy parts, we buy commodities, we

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:10.850
buy services, we buy transportation, we
buy everything.

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:14.450
So, if you don't buy well you can do

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:18.250
miracles on anywhere you want, you're not
going to prevail.

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:20.850
You need to make sure that your purchasing
process

00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:23.370
is in line, so number one, number one
priority.

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:26.280
Number two, number two priority making
sure,

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:29.190
for example, that you know exactly what
are

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:32.150
the segments, on the market where you have

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:33.990
the legitimacy so you can re-establish
your name.

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:37.220
You can re-establish the brand into this
particular segment.

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:41.240
So, you need, first, to, to establish what
are the priority, and then you say, okay.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:46.778
Why am I, even though I have a Keiretsu,
why my purchasing cost is much higher,

00:07:46.778 --> 00:07:52.480
than, for example, the Renault purchasing
cost, which was a smaller company?

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:52.750
Why?

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:56.360
Why Honda and Toyota is making so much
money, while Nissan, which is

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:58.120
having the same work force, the same

00:07:58.120 --> 00:08:00.820
engineer coming from the a, same
university.

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:03.140
Part of the same culture, did not get
results.

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:05.060
So little bit little, you go to a very
common

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:09.440
sense approach where you say, these are
the most important processes.

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:11.210
Let me go and see the people who are in

00:08:11.210 --> 00:08:14.950
charge of the process or around this
process, interview them.

00:08:14.950 --> 00:08:15.350
What's wrong?

00:08:15.350 --> 00:08:17.670
What do you think is wrong?

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:18.830
How can we fix it?

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:21.930
Okay, so obviously solutions are going to
come like this.

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:25.860
But, by listening to hundreds of people
into this process,

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:29.260
I can tell you that the solution comes
very clearly.

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:31.430
Now, people usually avoid the dramatic
solution.

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:33.490
For example,we had over capacity nobody
want

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:36.690
to talk about shutting down plants in
Japan.

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:39.650
Why, because it is something which is
sacred, no, you know shutting

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:44.088
down a plant in Japan is something that
nobody does, nobody do.

00:08:44.088 --> 00:08:47.370
I mean it's something that has to be
forced at a certain point in time.

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:48.658
Dismantling the [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:48.658 --> 00:08:50.550
Nobody's going to tell you [INAUDIBLE] is
not working.

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:54.235
People are going to tell you, yeah, we're
buying at the higher price.

00:08:54.235 --> 00:08:57.670
And then you go see the people in the
[INAUDIBLE] and they tell you

00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:02.940
why they have to sell Nissan at the higher
price, than Honda and Toyota.

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:05.780
So interviewing, asking question, in a
very simple way.

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:10.790
And then it's gonna look, it's gonna look
very clear.

00:09:10.790 --> 00:09:16.100
People contribute to it, they may not like
the final shape of the, of the plan.

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:17.720
But if you commit to results,

00:09:20.470 --> 00:09:20.920
everybody joined.

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:21.980
&gt;&gt; So it, I mean it may look like common

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:24.160
sense in retrospect but I, I think every
expert you

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:26.070
would have talked to at the time would
have said

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:28.330
hey, you know, there's the Japanese way of
doing business.

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:28.790
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:09:28.790 --> 00:09:30.090
&gt;&gt; And, and, and this is how things are
done.

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:31.480
And, and every class you would take in how

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:34.090
to do business in a global environment
would say you

00:09:34.090 --> 00:09:37.800
respect local cultures and, and, but how,
like what

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:40.070
gave you the confidence to, to challenge
the status quo?

00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:42.526
&gt;&gt; No, I am, I am not gonna challenge you
know,

00:09:42.526 --> 00:09:46.816
what you're learning at the university,
particularly telling you that, you know.

00:09:46.816 --> 00:09:49.596
But, but when you go and you are a
foreigner and you're

00:09:49.596 --> 00:09:53.300
going to Japan, you're going to Japan for
one reason, and that's it.

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:54.800
It's turning around the company, period.

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:59.870
That's what people are expecting from you.

00:09:59.870 --> 00:10:02.710
How much work, what do you do, if, if,

00:10:02.710 --> 00:10:08.870
if, if you have problems, if you have
remorse, empathy.

00:10:08.870 --> 00:10:09.860
Nobody cares.

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:11.610
Fix it.

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:11.880
That's it.

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:12.710
That's the message, fix it.

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:14.290
You accept the job, you have to fix it.

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:17.580
We had, two main problems.

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:20.305
One of the main problem of Nissan is that
we are

00:10:20.305 --> 00:10:24.830
$20 billion of debt in 1999, and no
Japanese bank, and

00:10:24.830 --> 00:10:27.420
you know how flexible Japanese bank can be
for a Japanese

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:31.460
company, no Japanese bank wanted to lend
any money to Nissan.

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:34.407
So we were facing a cash crunch.

00:10:34.407 --> 00:10:38.172
Okay, this is immediate death, okay.

00:10:38.172 --> 00:10:41.011
So we need to reestablish the thrust on it
and cut the

00:10:41.011 --> 00:10:44.325
debt, second you need to come back to
profit for ten years.

00:10:44.325 --> 00:10:48.685
Nissan had 1% average operating profit
while

00:10:48.685 --> 00:10:52.612
Honda and Toyota were around 7 to 10%.

00:10:52.612 --> 00:10:55.190
We had about 1% on return invested capital

00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:58.192
where our main competitors were more than
10%.

00:10:58.192 --> 00:10:59.113
There was something wrong.

00:10:59.113 --> 00:11:01.550
You need to re-establish, you re-establish
this.

00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:02.691
Put it, put it right.

00:11:02.691 --> 00:11:06.558
So I knew from the beginning that what was
expected from me is, fix it.

00:11:06.558 --> 00:11:07.026
Fix it.

00:11:07.026 --> 00:11:11.084
So if, if, when you know you need to fix
it, you, you can say,

00:11:11.084 --> 00:11:13.534
okay, I'm gonna make as many changes

00:11:13.534 --> 00:11:16.695
as necessary to fix the problem, nothing
more.

00:11:16.695 --> 00:11:17.480
Nothing more.

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:22.219
So changing the habits, if there is no
obvious results, you shouldn't do.

00:11:22.219 --> 00:11:27.973
But when you know that fixing the company
is gonna force you to change some habits.

00:11:27.973 --> 00:11:30.058
You need to say it, you need to explain
why

00:11:30.058 --> 00:11:32.741
you are doing it, and particularly you
need to commit

00:11:32.741 --> 00:11:35.661
to the results, because people don't like
change as long

00:11:35.661 --> 00:11:38.952
as there is no commitment to specific
results behind the change.

00:11:38.952 --> 00:11:42.343
Now if you commit to specific results,
they may give you the benefit

00:11:42.343 --> 00:11:46.390
of the doubt, and that's exactly what
happened in the case of Nissan.

00:11:46.390 --> 00:11:49.500
As you know I spent three months building
the plant listening to the

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:52.980
people, and everybody told me we cannot
shut down the plant in Japan.

00:11:52.980 --> 00:11:56.653
You cannot dismantle the Keiretsu in, in
in Japan.

00:11:56.653 --> 00:11:59.828
You cannot challenge the seniority system
in Japan.

00:11:59.828 --> 00:12:04.310
You cannot put younger people in top jobs
in Japan, diversity, etcetera, etcetera.

00:12:04.310 --> 00:12:07.720
I mean the list of, you cannot do this and
that, was huge.

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:10.800
So I had two choices, first choice I
listen to all

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:14.230
of this, then the consequence is, I take
back the family.

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:15.280
I take back my bag.

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:18.520
I go back to France, because there was
nothing I could do.

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:19.710
The second one is, I need to fix it.

00:12:19.710 --> 00:12:23.380
Then, I need to say, okay, I cannot do
everything,

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:27.700
but let's do the changes that are gonna
deliver results.

00:12:27.700 --> 00:12:29.020
So I have dismantled the Keiretsu of
Nissan.

00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:31.710
Not because I consider the Keiretsu was a
wrong

00:12:31.710 --> 00:12:35.110
solution, but I said the Keiretsu didn't
work for Nissan.

00:12:35.110 --> 00:12:37.380
It's working for Toyota.

00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:38.260
It's working for Honda.

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:38.670
Good for them.

00:12:38.670 --> 00:12:39.610
It didn't work for us.

00:12:39.610 --> 00:12:40.960
That's why we're changing it.

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:45.460
I challenge the seniority system by
saying, it's not I'm, I'm, I

00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:49.200
have nothing about people who are 50 or 60
or 70 years old.

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:53.220
But I don't want a segregation against
younger Japanese people who have a lot

00:12:53.220 --> 00:12:56.052
of talent, and who cannot access the

00:12:56.052 --> 00:12:58.390
jobs of responsibility, even though they
deserve them.

00:12:58.390 --> 00:13:00.120
So I turn it around.

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:03.870
I, I consider that we need to stop the
segregation against

00:13:03.870 --> 00:13:07.490
young people into the system, and always
with a specific objective.

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:11.390
Keiretsu, again dismantling the cross
shareholding with many companies.

00:13:11.390 --> 00:13:14.000
I've done everything but every single
time, explain why.

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:17.510
And particularly the most important thing,
I committed to resign.

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:21.550
If one year after we started the plan, the
company would not be in the black, and I

00:13:21.550 --> 00:13:25.548
said we're gonna divide the debt of $20
billion

00:13:25.548 --> 00:13:30.700
dollars by, by two within the, within
three years term.

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:35.840
And that the company growth would resume
after having 10 years of decline.

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:39.390
And, I said if any one of these objectives
is not met, I'm out.

00:13:39.390 --> 00:13:41.530
And, obviously with me, all the members of
the executive company.

00:13:41.530 --> 00:13:44.482
So, this commitment, which was very
strong, because this was

00:13:44.482 --> 00:13:46.448
not a commitment in a board room, this was
a

00:13:46.448 --> 00:13:48.906
commitment on TV in front of the public,
so everybody

00:13:48.906 --> 00:13:51.916
knew that, you're gonna have to deliver,
or get out.

00:13:51.916 --> 00:13:56.445
give, give me and give us the benefit of
the doubt.

00:13:56.445 --> 00:13:59.326
The rest of the story, you know it.

00:13:59.326 --> 00:14:00.928
&gt;&gt; [COUGH] So, I mean, Nissan had

00:14:00.928 --> 00:14:03.686
gone through many strategic plans
previously and I

00:14:03.686 --> 00:14:06.442
think you famously said, strategy is five
percent

00:14:06.442 --> 00:14:08.833
of the answer and execution is, is 95%.

00:14:08.833 --> 00:14:12.879
So, so what does that mean, and how did
you actually, what did you do differently?

00:14:12.879 --> 00:14:16.116
&gt;&gt; Well, you know the, the five percent
yeah, I said, yeah,

00:14:16.116 --> 00:14:20.133
five percent of the challenge is the
strategy and 95% is the execution.

00:14:20.133 --> 00:14:25.177
This, I said it as CO of Nissan, I said it
also as CO of [UNKNOWN] and I said it

00:14:25.177 --> 00:14:31.060
as deputy CO of [UNKNOWN] when I said, the
first time, it was mainly in France.

00:14:31.060 --> 00:14:35.831
And I was to my, to the top management of
[UNKNOWN] in France.

00:14:35.831 --> 00:14:39.085
Because there was a tendency in the
company to spend too

00:14:39.085 --> 00:14:43.171
much time on strategy and leave the
execution for other people.

00:14:43.171 --> 00:14:45.528
And this is where I said, yeah, strategies

00:14:45.528 --> 00:14:49.038
fine, it's very important, but it's only
five percent.

00:14:49.038 --> 00:14:52.063
If you don't go to execution after this
the, the delivery

00:14:52.063 --> 00:14:54.794
and the promise of the company is gonna be
very weak.

00:14:54.794 --> 00:14:56.463
I really, I really believe it.

00:14:56.463 --> 00:15:00.467
Now it doesn't mean that strategy is not
important, but when you establish North.

00:15:00.467 --> 00:15:04.321
And you know exactly where north is for
the company.

00:15:04.321 --> 00:15:07.410
You've done something very important
because you're giving the direction.

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:10.660
But if you don't start moving North, if
you don't start

00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:13.880
mapping, and establishing milestone,
you're never,

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:15.270
you're never gonna be getting there.

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:17.080
You're never gonna be getting there.

00:15:17.080 --> 00:15:20.970
You know, in the car industry, if you
noticed, when you see the evolution

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:24.790
of our industry for the last 30 years,
there have been a tremendous change.

00:15:24.790 --> 00:15:30.673
A lot of companies which were number one,
number two, number three worldwide

00:15:30.673 --> 00:15:35.780
downgraded and weakened and a lot of
newcomers coming up.

00:15:35.780 --> 00:15:38.910
If, when you take a look to the new,
leaders

00:15:38.910 --> 00:15:41.740
of the industry, all of them have one
thing in common.

00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:43.515
Obviously, you have the Japanese, you have
Germans,

00:15:43.515 --> 00:15:46.360
you have Koreans, you have
Franco-Japanese, you have everything.

00:15:46.360 --> 00:15:49.560
There is one thing in common, is the

00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:54.680
discipline of execution and the importance
of processes.

00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:58.090
In our industry, at the end of the day,
yes, strategy is important.

00:15:58.090 --> 00:16:02.570
But at the end of the day, the most
disciplined organization, the,

00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:07.530
the organization which gives a lot of
importance to processes end up prevailing.

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:10.680
Who's number one in our industry today?

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:10.870
Toyota.

00:16:10.870 --> 00:16:11.720
Who's number two?

00:16:11.720 --> 00:16:12.220
Volkswagen.

00:16:14.300 --> 00:16:16.877
Who's number three depending if you
consider volume or revenues.

00:16:16.877 --> 00:16:17.967
Let's say it's between General Motors and
ourself, Renault Nissan.

00:16:17.967 --> 00:16:18.968
Who's number five?

00:16:18.968 --> 00:16:19.316
Hyundai.

00:16:19.316 --> 00:16:19.849
Hyundia Kia.

00:16:19.849 --> 00:16:22.200
What a change compared to 20 years ago.

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:27.149
When you take a look to the classification
of this industry

00:16:27.149 --> 00:16:32.410
20 years ago, it's a complete, it's a
complete change.

00:16:32.410 --> 00:16:36.050
What's in common for at least four out of
these five top companies?

00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:43.550
Big discipline in execution, a lot of im,
importance given to processes.

00:16:43.550 --> 00:16:47.225
It's not sufficient, but this is where the
difference is being made.

00:16:47.225 --> 00:16:51.020
&gt;&gt; [COUGH] One of the stories I, I liked
for your initial arrival in, in Japan

00:16:51.020 --> 00:16:52.690
was you decided the executives would've
been too

00:16:52.690 --> 00:16:55.540
far removed from, from the product, from
the

00:16:57.920 --> 00:16:58.830
car.

00:16:58.830 --> 00:17:01.010
And so you moved your executive meetings
to the test track.

00:17:01.010 --> 00:17:02.060
Can you talk about that a little bit?

00:17:02.060 --> 00:17:04.217
&gt;&gt; Oh, that, that's, I mean, our industry
is an industry

00:17:04.217 --> 00:17:06.726
around the product, and everything starts
from there and ends up here.

00:17:06.726 --> 00:17:09.950
It's, it's, I don't think you can consider
anybody

00:17:09.950 --> 00:17:13.619
to be at the top of our industry without

00:17:16.180 --> 00:17:19.730
a strong contribution in term of product
whatever it is,

00:17:19.730 --> 00:17:23.550
technology or quality, or reliability or a
perceived quality, or pricing.

00:17:23.550 --> 00:17:24.720
Everything is around the product.

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:29.181
So, understanding the product is
absolutely fundamental.

00:17:29.181 --> 00:17:31.698
And, asking top management in our

00:17:31.698 --> 00:17:34.687
industry, no matter what is their
function.

00:17:34.687 --> 00:17:38.431
They can be key financial officer, they
can be specialists of HR.

00:17:38.431 --> 00:17:40.654
They don't have to be engineers and
especially have a good product.

00:17:40.654 --> 00:17:43.992
But the fact that they put themselves in
the

00:17:43.992 --> 00:17:47.793
shoes of the consumer, listen to the, the,
the

00:17:47.793 --> 00:17:53.450
advantages, the weaknesses, the
opportunities, comparing our

00:17:53.450 --> 00:17:58.885
product to the one of the, of competitors
is absolutely essential.

00:17:58.885 --> 00:18:00.720
[COUGH] So maybe back to what you talked
about

00:18:00.720 --> 00:18:03.450
for discipline, at Michigan, sorry, in
Michelin, your second job

00:18:03.450 --> 00:18:05.090
out of training was, as the dean said,
manager of

00:18:05.090 --> 00:18:07.830
a factory that, that made huge tires for
heavy equipment.

00:18:07.830 --> 00:18:09.860
I checked our most recent employment
report, and, and

00:18:09.860 --> 00:18:13.490
there were no factory managers, leaving
Stanford Business School.

00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:15.900
Are there things that you learned there
that, that affect your

00:18:15.900 --> 00:18:20.650
decision making process as the CEO of two
global manufacturing companies?

00:18:20.650 --> 00:18:21.000
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:23.280
I mean, I, I I consider that the,

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:27.384
what we call manufacturing today or mon,
the monozukuri.

00:18:27.384 --> 00:18:28.761
I use a Japanese term of monozukuri.

00:18:28.761 --> 00:18:31.360
Monozukuri is everything which covers,

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:33.480
product planning, engineering,
manufacturing, supply

00:18:36.570 --> 00:18:37.560
chain.

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:41.740
This is all the area of the company where
the product is born.

00:18:41.740 --> 00:18:42.176
Okay.

00:18:42.176 --> 00:18:46.120
Versus marketing distributing, selling,
financing, et cetera.

00:18:47.120 --> 00:18:50.590
This area of the company, particularly for
products which are

00:18:50.590 --> 00:18:52.000
not commodities, products which are

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:54.420
changing all the time, absolutely
essential.

00:18:54.420 --> 00:18:56.970
And having, starting your carrier, in my

00:18:56.970 --> 00:18:59.970
opinion, starting your carrier in this
area where

00:18:59.970 --> 00:19:03.900
you are at the crossroad of many

00:19:03.900 --> 00:19:07.600
things turning around the product is
absolutely essential.

00:19:07.600 --> 00:19:11.800
I wanted to be in manufacturing because in
manufacturing, first you deal with people.

00:19:11.800 --> 00:19:13.540
Second, you deal with the product.

00:19:13.540 --> 00:19:14.860
Third, you deal with the supplier.

00:19:14.860 --> 00:19:16.000
Then you deal with quality.

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:17.500
So everything in one place.

00:19:17.500 --> 00:19:21.760
And manufacturing the plant is, the on,
the first place where the product is born.

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:24.360
Before the manufacturing, the product is
just a fiction.

00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:25.390
It's on a computer.

00:19:25.390 --> 00:19:28.560
It's on a design, but the first

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:31.970
place where the product exists is in
manufacturing.

00:19:31.970 --> 00:19:34.710
So I wanted to start in manufacturing, and
frankly, I don't, I don't regret it.

00:19:34.710 --> 00:19:38.540
But I can tell you some of our top
executives

00:19:38.540 --> 00:19:41.270
both in Renault and Nissan, not all of
them, but some

00:19:41.270 --> 00:19:45.485
of the top executives, often come from a
background of

00:19:45.485 --> 00:19:49.961
what we call monozukuri, and a lot of them
from manufacturing.

00:19:49.961 --> 00:19:53.190
&gt;&gt; [COUGH] So, going on perhaps a decade,
of

00:19:53.190 --> 00:19:56.410
running simultaneously two, two global
companies, how do you

00:19:56.410 --> 00:19:58.240
get the stamina to, to keep going and to

00:19:58.240 --> 00:20:01.360
stay fit, you know, personally and, and
mentally and physically?

00:20:01.360 --> 00:20:03.210
Well, you know, [COUGH] somebody said,
with

00:20:03.210 --> 00:20:04.290
a little discipline, you do little things.

00:20:04.290 --> 00:20:06.210
With a lot of discipline, you do a lot of
things,

00:20:06.210 --> 00:20:11.970
and with total discipline, there is
nothing which you cannot do.

00:20:11.970 --> 00:20:14.140
There was a lot of discipline.

00:20:14.140 --> 00:20:16.900
I mean, obviously, because you're gonna
move from one word to

00:20:16.900 --> 00:20:22.830
the other, from one culture to the other,
from completely different company.

00:20:22.830 --> 00:20:26.510
You have jet lags, organization, people
are expecting you when you come down

00:20:26.510 --> 00:20:31.170
from a plane after 14 hours flight to be
fresh and ready for them.

00:20:31.170 --> 00:20:33.891
They're [LAUGH] they're not expecting the
CEO to come and say,

00:20:33.891 --> 00:20:36.370
oh my God, this jet lag he cannot answer a
question.

00:20:36.370 --> 00:20:40.090
They want you to be on the go all the
time.

00:20:40.090 --> 00:20:42.150
So this requires a lot of discipline.

00:20:42.150 --> 00:20:43.490
You have to organize yourself.

00:20:43.490 --> 00:20:45.850
If, if you know you're gonna do this job
and you accept

00:20:45.850 --> 00:20:49.300
the job, you have to organize yourself in
order to do it.

00:20:49.300 --> 00:20:51.080
It's tough, but there is a solution.

00:20:51.080 --> 00:20:52.558
There is, there is a solution for it.

00:20:52.558 --> 00:20:55.280
But it requires lot of choices, personal
choices.

00:20:55.280 --> 00:20:57.600
There are a lot of things you cannot do,
you know.

00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:01.400
There are a lot of things you can do, but
you gonna do them within a certain order.

00:21:01.400 --> 00:21:02.119
And, but,

00:21:06.210 --> 00:21:07.600
particularly with a lot, with

00:21:09.910 --> 00:21:10.430
a lot of discipline.

00:21:11.430 --> 00:21:15.650
&gt;&gt; Can you give us some examples of, of
how you, how you've done that personally?

00:21:15.650 --> 00:21:17.600
&gt;&gt; You know, I can, I can give you I can
give you my, my program

00:21:17.600 --> 00:21:17.650
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:21:17.650 --> 00:21:19.130
&gt;&gt; The most simple thing is give you my

00:21:19.130 --> 00:21:21.220
program for this week and next week, for
example.

00:21:21.220 --> 00:21:22.636
I mean just not to go.

00:21:22.636 --> 00:21:25.495
Yesterday I was, into the pre inauguration
of

00:21:25.495 --> 00:21:28.890
a new plant that Nissan is building in
Brazil.

00:21:28.890 --> 00:21:31.470
So a lot of people are gonna say, yeah,
lucky you.

00:21:31.470 --> 00:21:34.079
You were in Rio Janeiro/ You, you, you,
there's no complaint.

00:21:34.079 --> 00:21:35.640
[LAUGH] But I was in Rio Janeiro.

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:38.897
In, in fact in the state of Rio Janeiro in
Resende, I reviewed

00:21:38.897 --> 00:21:42.730
the new plant of Nissan, which is going to
be inaugurated in April.

00:21:42.730 --> 00:21:45.850
So I was spend the day in Resende.

00:21:46.950 --> 00:21:48.360
Took the plane, flew, I am here today.

00:21:48.360 --> 00:21:54.250
Later I'm going to our, research center,
that we have opened in, Silicon Valley in

00:21:54.250 --> 00:21:56.440
order to make sure that we understand what

00:21:56.440 --> 00:22:02.020
the startup are doing, particular around
autonomous driving.

00:22:02.020 --> 00:22:03.590
We're, we're gonna probably talk about it.

00:22:03.590 --> 00:22:06.879
So, Wednesday night, tomorrow night, I'm
going to our, headqua,

00:22:06.879 --> 00:22:10.150
headquarter in the United States, which is
based in Nashville.

00:22:10.150 --> 00:22:11.580
We're gonna have a review with all

00:22:11.580 --> 00:22:14.930
the North American team about, about the
performance.

00:22:14.930 --> 00:22:17.970
I'm gonna visit Kenton, Mississippi
Friday, which is

00:22:17.970 --> 00:22:21.440
one of our large plant, on, on, on Friday.

00:22:21.440 --> 00:22:23.079
Then I'm flying for Japan.

00:22:23.079 --> 00:22:25.045
Next week I would be Monday in Tokyo.

00:22:25.045 --> 00:22:32.484
I would be in Tokyo from Monday to,
Friday, executive committee, board

00:22:32.484 --> 00:22:39.943
meeting, corporate officer meetings,
investment committee, et cetera.

00:22:39.943 --> 00:22:46.150
You go through all the elements, and then
I'm flying for Paris.

00:22:46.150 --> 00:22:46.788
I have my schedule for 2014 booked
already.

00:22:46.788 --> 00:22:47.180
That's so we [LAUGH].

00:22:47.180 --> 00:22:48.740
Now you can't, I mean, you, you have to
accept that.

00:22:48.740 --> 00:22:51.568
I mean, I know exactly where I'm gonna be
hopefully, [LAUGH] in December 2014.

00:22:51.568 --> 00:22:56.116
All the board meeting of both company
already

00:22:56.116 --> 00:23:01.509
set, executive committee, the visit to the
plant.

00:23:01.509 --> 00:23:03.477
Everything has to be organized.

00:23:03.477 --> 00:23:06.518
If not, there is no way you can do
everything you have, have to do.

00:23:06.518 --> 00:23:08.030
From time to time, you have accidents.

00:23:08.030 --> 00:23:12.186
From time to time, there are accidents,
but fortunately, I'm surrounded by

00:23:12.186 --> 00:23:16.690
professional people who can adapt to the
situation and make the necessary changes.

00:23:16.690 --> 00:23:18.670
You know, how do you do it?

00:23:18.670 --> 00:23:21.310
It's possible, but it requires a lot of
discipline.

00:23:23.270 --> 00:23:24.880
&gt;&gt; You think there are any, any
fundamentals about

00:23:24.880 --> 00:23:28.380
leadership that, that transcend a specific
company or industry?

00:23:28.380 --> 00:23:29.793
I mean, do you have a personal leadership
philosophy?

00:23:29.793 --> 00:23:31.290
[COUGH].

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:33.780
&gt;&gt; yeah, but, you know, leadership also
depends on the time.

00:23:35.156 --> 00:23:38.480
I don't think if you're a good leader
today, you're gonna be a

00:23:38.480 --> 00:23:41.820
great leader ten years down the road or 20
years down the road.

00:23:41.820 --> 00:23:43.610
And I don't think that the great leader of

00:23:43.610 --> 00:23:45.643
today would have been a great leader 20
years ago.

00:23:45.643 --> 00:23:47.930
I think it's adapt to the time.

00:23:47.930 --> 00:23:53.370
If I take today and the foreseeable
future, there are some attributes, in my

00:23:53.370 --> 00:23:59.220
opinion, which are absolutely necessary
for leadership position,

00:23:59.220 --> 00:24:05.270
particularly for companies but not only
for company.

00:24:05.270 --> 00:24:06.440
And the number one, the number one
attribute, I think,

00:24:06.440 --> 00:24:09.400
is what I call, empathy, the capacity to
connect with people.

00:24:09.400 --> 00:24:12.550
That's extremely important.

00:24:12.550 --> 00:24:15.915
Why I am telling you this because, you
know, I, when I

00:24:15.915 --> 00:24:21.200
look at the alliance, we have about
450,000 people working in the alliance.

00:24:22.300 --> 00:24:26.640
Our first group of people, are Russian.

00:24:26.640 --> 00:24:30.430
This is the largest number of employees we
have.

00:24:30.430 --> 00:24:30.760
Are Russians.

00:24:30.760 --> 00:24:35.130
The second largest group of employees we
have is Chinese.

00:24:35.130 --> 00:24:40.393
The third largest group we have is
Japanese, and the fourth is French.

00:24:40.393 --> 00:24:42.454
And then you continue like this, and

00:24:42.454 --> 00:24:45.325
you have American, you have British, et
cetera.

00:24:45.325 --> 00:24:50.368
And when you have 450,000 people, with
very large group of people spread all

00:24:50.368 --> 00:24:52.926
over the world, the capacity to connect,

00:24:52.926 --> 00:24:55.840
the capacity to understand, the capacity
to get

00:24:55.840 --> 00:24:59.392
out from your own reality and try to see
what's on the mind of

00:24:59.392 --> 00:25:01.452
your own people who are fighting on

00:25:01.452 --> 00:25:04.823
different fronts is, is for me absolutely
essential.

00:25:04.823 --> 00:25:09.026
And is gonna continue to be essential for
many years to come.

00:25:09.026 --> 00:25:10.494
This is basic attribute.

00:25:10.494 --> 00:25:15.438
Frankly, if, if I find somebody in my team
and he has no empathy, he doesn't have

00:25:15.438 --> 00:25:18.303
the capacity to connect, I think it's
gonna be

00:25:18.303 --> 00:25:21.800
very hard for him, to be in leadership
position.

00:25:21.800 --> 00:25:26.072
Now, being emp, having this empathy, this
capacity to connect is not sufficient.

00:25:26.072 --> 00:25:30.870
I'm saying it's a basic and absolutely
necessary condition for leadership.

00:25:30.870 --> 00:25:35.730
Obviously, you have other attribute, but
you're asking me some of them.

00:25:35.730 --> 00:25:41.070
I'm telling you I consider empathy is, is
the, probably the most important really.

00:25:41.070 --> 00:25:42.770
&gt;&gt; And I think you already said this.

00:25:42.770 --> 00:25:45.095
Would you say that decisiveness is number
two.

00:25:45.095 --> 00:25:45.894
Is that, I mean, you've
&gt;&gt; What was the second one?

00:25:45.894 --> 00:25:47.227
&gt;&gt; Decisiveness you talked about, like,
the

00:25:47.227 --> 00:25:48.700
flip side of empathy and, and
decisiveness.

00:25:48.700 --> 00:25:50.170
&gt;&gt; Yeah decisiveness is number two,

00:25:50.170 --> 00:25:53.005
but decisiveness doesn't mean a quick
decision.

00:25:53.005 --> 00:25:58.690
Decisiveness mean adequate decision at the
right time, and

00:25:58.690 --> 00:26:00.153
the right time may not be the quick time.

00:26:00.153 --> 00:26:03.240
You know, particularly that is something I
learned in Asia, because obviously

00:26:03.240 --> 00:26:06.999
when you take a CEO job in a company, you
learn a lot.

00:26:06.999 --> 00:26:08.070
You learn a lot.

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:12.410
One of the thing I learned particularly in
Japan that from time to time making quick

00:26:12.410 --> 00:26:16.000
decision, is gonna hurt the execution of
the

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:17.540
decision because, I think, what do you
want?

00:26:17.540 --> 00:26:19.040
When you make a decision, what do you
want?

00:26:19.040 --> 00:26:20.510
You want to transform reality.

00:26:20.510 --> 00:26:24.180
You don't want to make a decision for the
sake of making a decision.

00:26:24.180 --> 00:26:29.870
You make a decision because you want
something to change.

00:26:29.870 --> 00:26:31.550
So, your goal is the thing changed.

00:26:31.550 --> 00:26:32.170
That's your goal.

00:26:32.170 --> 00:26:34.810
So the question now is, when should you
make the decision in order

00:26:34.810 --> 00:26:39.100
to make sure that this thing has changed
in the fastest, period of time.

00:26:39.100 --> 00:26:41.670
So from time to time, you have to wait

00:26:41.670 --> 00:26:45.650
to make the decision because you're
preparing people to execute.

00:26:45.650 --> 00:26:47.780
From time to time, you need to make the
decision very early.

00:26:47.780 --> 00:26:49.430
From time to time, a little bit late.

00:26:49.430 --> 00:26:53.360
But when I say decisiveness mean capacity
to take the decision at the appropriate

00:26:53.360 --> 00:26:59.490
time because at the end of the day, what
you need is, reality changes.

00:26:59.490 --> 00:27:00.779
That's, that's your goal.

00:27:00.779 --> 00:27:04.650
&gt;&gt; So, [COUGH] I know we promised we'd
